+| Keep reading | 9/10 | Chapter 13, the stranger entering the ten-degree blind spot; chapter 15, Havel choosing not to raise the unsigned marker. | The movement resolves its training arguments while widening institutional and personal danger. Havel's choice is quiet but consequential. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 11, the betting man reads Lira's change before the official books do; chapter 13, Cael changes his heading to **“What it's made of.”** | The story links combat analysis to markets, records, bureaucracy, and identity. Those systems disagree in interesting ways instead of serving as decorative worldbuilding. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 8/10 | Chapter 12, six-phase explanation and gaze measurement; chapter 13, reconstruction of Dessa, the river-academy man, and Ulric. | Ideas are lucid at sentence level and physically demonstrated. The chief weakness is conceptual reiteration: the text sometimes supplies a third proof after the reader has accepted the claim. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 10, Lira explains the locked gate and her ambition; chapter 15, Cael understands **“I like it here”** only after saying it. | Ambition, belonging, and shame remain grounded in choices. Cael and Lira change one another without collapsing into the same person. Havel also becomes sympathetic quickly. | High |
